1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:00,700
<u>Architecture</u>

2
00:00:02,033 --> 00:00:03,323
<i>What is ViewModel used for?</i>

3
00:00:04,594 --> 00:00:09,914
A ViewModel stores and coordinates screen state, survives configuration changes, and separates UI from data orchestration.

4
00:00:10,614 --> 00:00:13,424
It should not depend directly on a View, Fragment, or Activity.

5
00:00:14,937 --> 00:00:16,277
<i>How should UI state be stored?</i>

6
00:00:17,787 --> 00:00:22,067
Usually as StateFlow<UiState>, where UiState is an immutable data class or a sealed hierarchy.

7
00:00:22,777 --> 00:00:24,597
The UI observes the state and renders it.

8
00:00:26,113 --> 00:00:27,843
<i>How should state and effects be separated?</i>

9
00:00:29,371 --> 00:00:34,081
State describes what the screen currently shows, while effects are one-time actions such as navigation or a snackbar.

10
00:00:34,791 --> 00:00:39,691
State is usually stored in StateFlow, while effects are delivered through SharedFlow, Channel, or another explicit model.

11
00:00:41,225 --> 00:00:42,625
<i>What is a single source of truth?</i>

12
00:00:44,142 --> 00:00:46,742
It is a principle where data or state has one primary owner.

13
00:00:47,452 --> 00:00:50,922
Other parts of the system receive it from that owner instead of keeping competing copies.

14
00:00:52,449 --> 00:00:54,019
<i>Is a domain layer always required?</i>

15
00:00:55,563 --> 00:00:56,033
No.

16
00:00:56,733 --> 00:01:01,573
It is useful for complex business logic, reusable use cases, and separating rules from UI and data layers,

17
00:01:01,783 --> 00:01:03,773
but it can be unnecessary for simple CRUD flows.

18
00:01:05,281 --> 00:01:06,771
<i>What is a Repository used for?</i>

19
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:12,410
A Repository hides concrete data sources and exposes one contract to the UI or domain layer.

20
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:15,880
It can coordinate network, database, cache, and mapping.

21
00:01:17,753 --> 00:01:21,313
<i>How would you design loading data from an API and saving it to a local database?</i>

22
00:01:22,594 --> 00:01:26,914
A Repository coordinates remote and local data sources: it loads DTOs, maps them to entities,

23
00:01:27,044 --> 00:01:28,544
and saves them to Room in a transaction.

24
00:01:29,244 --> 00:01:33,104
The UI observes Room through Flow as the single source of truth; refresh policy, stale data,

25
00:01:33,334 --> 00:01:35,104
and error behavior must be defined separately.

26
00:01:36,614 --> 00:01:37,914
<i>MVVM or MVI?</i>

27
00:01:39,435 --> 00:01:42,235
MVVM is usually simpler and works well for most screens.

28
00:01:42,935 --> 00:01:48,665
MVI is useful for complex state-driven UI where explicit actions, immutable state, and predictable transitions are important.

29
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:52,190
<i>Is a separate framework required for MVI?</i>

30
00:01:53,702 --> 00:01:54,172
No.

31
00:01:54,872 --> 00:02:00,562
An MVI-style approach can be implemented with a regular ViewModel, StateFlow, immutable UiState, and sealed actions or effects.

32
00:02:02,086 --> 00:02:02,956
<i>What is a reducer?</i>

33
00:02:04,470 --> 00:02:09,160
A reducer computes a new state from the previous state and an action or result: State + Action -> New State.

34
00:02:09,870 --> 00:02:12,120
In a strict Redux-style design it is a pure function,

35
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:14,450
which makes transitions predictable and easy to test.

36
00:02:15,969 --> 00:02:18,379
<i>Is a reducer only a Redux or MVI concept?</i>

37
00:02:19,894 --> 00:02:20,364
No.

38
00:02:21,064 --> 00:02:26,534
The term is historically associated with Flux and Redux and is common in MVI, but MVVM can also use immutable UiState,

39
00:02:26,704 --> 00:02:28,264
actions and reducer-like transitions.

40
00:02:28,974 --> 00:02:30,814
Not every screen needs a formal reducer.

41
00:02:32,689 --> 00:02:35,289
<i>What does reducer-like state management mean in modern Android?</i>

42
00:02:36,546 --> 00:02:42,576
It means updating immutable state through explicit transitions, often directly in a ViewModel with _state.update { it.copy(...) }.

43
00:02:43,266 --> 00:02:47,596
The code gains reducer benefits without requiring a dedicated Reducer class or Redux-style framework.

44
00:02:49,097 --> 00:02:50,277
<i>What is Clean Architecture?</i>

45
00:02:51,798 --> 00:02:56,888
It is an approach that separates UI, business logic, and data access and points dependencies toward more stable abstractions.

46
00:02:57,588 --> 00:03:00,718
It should be applied pragmatically rather than adding layers without real value.

47
00:03:02,241 --> 00:03:05,191
<i>What criteria should be used to split an application into modules?</i>

48
00:03:07,054 --> 00:03:10,504
Modules should be separated by functional responsibility, dependency direction,

49
00:03:10,624 --> 00:03:12,324
independent development, and reuse.

50
00:03:13,034 --> 00:03:16,934
Typical boundaries are features, design system, networking, storage, and shared contracts;

51
00:03:17,074 --> 00:03:19,024
each module should expose a clear public API.

52
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:23,020
<i>What is the difference between api and implementation in Gradle?</i>

53
00:03:24,523 --> 00:03:29,433
implementation keeps the dependency internal to the current module and does not add it to the consumers' compile classpath.

54
00:03:30,143 --> 00:03:35,133
api exposes it transitively, so it is used when types from that dependency appear in the module's public API.

55
00:03:36,665 --> 00:03:38,365
<i>Why use a multi-module architecture?</i>

56
00:03:39,899 --> 00:03:44,009
It helps separate features, reduce coupling, improve ownership, and speed up incremental builds.

57
00:03:44,719 --> 00:03:48,029
However, splitting too early increases Gradle complexity and boilerplate.

58
00:03:49,545 --> 00:03:51,035
<i>How should legacy code be handled?</i>

59
00:03:52,558 --> 00:03:55,698
First, capture the current behavior, then make small and safe changes.

60
00:03:56,408 --> 00:04:01,277
Incremental refactoring is usually safer than a full rewrite because it preserves hidden business rules and reduces risk.
